body:
I don't have enough context on this feature to comment deeply, but at first glance I didn't think it was terrible (modulo the few spec bugs I filed around Touch etc.).  In particular, I realized it was probably convenient to be able to get certain built-in / CSS behaviors from the elements that back the canvas (eg. mouseenter/leave events, :hover states).  But importantly this API was the output of a bunch of canvas/accessibility experts (eg. Frank Oliver) who should probably be included in any discussion of "radical improvements".  Unfortunately they tell me on private-email that (since they work for Microsoft) their lawyers won't let them participate via WHATWG.

@smaug---- Do you want to propose something more radical than just the tweaks @junov suggests?  If so, would you mind doing that [here](https://github.com/w3c/2dcontext) so we can get feedback from the right people?  I know this is really stupid, but pragmatically we're really not going to ship any major change to an API in blink that Microsoft was involved in designing without doing whatever it takes to include them in the discussion.

Alternately, @junov your suggested change sounds reasonable to me, do you want to follow-up with Frank offline and see what he thinks and circle back here?
